<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Métis</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Métis people</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Métis are an Indigenous people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> who arose from the unions of fur traders, mostly French and Scottish, with First Nations women. Centred on the Red River valley, they developed a distinct culture, the Michif language and the buffalo hunt, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-riel">Louis Riel</a> resisted Canadian expansion in 1869–1870 and 1885.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/metis.jpg" alt="Métis" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown Métis resistance fighters, made official by Métis National Council · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Metis_Blue.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the Wolseley Expedition to Red River in 1870 after the resistance led by Louis Riel, and the suppression of the North-West Rebellion of 1885, when troops moved west on the unfinished Canadian Pacific Railway defeated the <b>Métis</b> at the Battle of Batoche; Riel was hanged later that year.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canada">Canada · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Métis</b> of western Canada and the French heritage of Louisiana, together with the Cajuns descended from Acadians exiled after 1755, are further legacies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-france">New France · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…five Canadian provinces before 1896, which led to the idea that it was made at the time of the Wolseley Expedition of 1870 to the Red River Valley of Manitoba, as the lament of a local woman, perhaps a <b>Métis</b>, whose soldier lover was about to return east.</blockquote><a href="/songs/red-river-valley">Red River Valley</a></li><li><blockquote>As they moved west onto the plains and took up horses and bison hunting, Saulteaux and later <b>Métis</b> groups joined them in an alliance the Cree call Nehiyaw-Pwat.</blockquote><a href="/state/iron-confederacy">Iron Confederacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Alarmed by Canadian surveyors, the <b>Métis</b> formed a National Committee, turned back the incoming lieutenant governor, William McDougall, and in November seized Upper Fort Garry.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-assiniboia">Provisional Government of Assiniboia</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Métis</b> families who had moved west from Red River to the South Saskatchewan River after 1870 feared losing their river lots to surveys, while Cree nations faced hunger after the buffalo vanished.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-saskatchewan">Provisional Government of Saskatchewan</a></li></ul></section>
